In Java, `System.out.println()` does not return any value, it is a void method. It simply prints the string or other data you pass to it to the console, followed by a newline character. 

The 'println' in `System.out.println()` stands for "print line." It is a part of Java's `PrintStream` class. Sometimes people get confused with `System.out.print()` which is similar to `System.out.println()`, but it does not append a newline character at the end.

Here's an example:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, World!");  // Prints 'Hello, World!' and goes to the next line
        System.out.print("Hello, World!");     // Prints 'Hello, World!' but does not go to the next line
    }
}
```

